<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tristán de Luna y Arellano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1510">1510</a>–<a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tristán de Luna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tristán de Luna y Arellano was a Spanish conquistador who in 1559 led a large expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> to found a colony on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> Bay in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. A hurricane soon destroyed most of his ships and supplies, and detachments sent inland in search of food reached the declining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a> of Coosa in 1560. The settlement was abandoned in 1561, after Luna had been replaced as governor.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tristán de Luna y Arellano was born in Borobia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, to a noble family. He was a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a>, viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, and of Juana de Zúñiga, the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> he served with Francisco Vásquez de Coronado on the expedition to the Seven Cities of Cíbola and put down an Indian rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Luís de Velasco, viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, chose him to found a settlement on the Gulf Coast and to open an overland route to Santa Elena, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, where a second outpost was planned. His fleet of eleven ships carried more than 1,500 soldiers and settlers under six captains of cavalry and six of infantry. Passing over Filipina Bay, modern Mobile Bay, it settled on Ochuse Bay, modern Pensacola Bay, and in late August and September 1559 founded Santa María de Ochuse.</p><p>On the night of 19 September 1559, before the ships had been unloaded, a hurricane with a storm surge struck the anchorage. Most of the men moved inland to the abandoned village of Nanipacana on the Alabama River, which they renamed Santa Cruz de Nanipacana. Relief ships sent in November carried the colony through the winter, but the supplies promised for the spring did not come, and hunger drove Luna to send a detachment of 200 men up to the Coosa chiefdom in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where it stayed until November.</p><p>Luna proved an ineffective leader, and tension grew between him and his officers and men. The viceroy replaced him with Ángel de Villafañe, who reached Pensacola Bay in April 1561. Luna was allowed to leave by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, but he spent the rest of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Luna died on 16 September 1573. Europeans did not settle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> area again until the Spanish founded Presidio Santa Maria de Galve in 1698. Archaeologists of the University of West Florida have identified a site of about 13 to 15 hectares as his settlement, the largest Spanish site of the mid-sixteenth century in the Southeast and the earliest European settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to last several years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tristán de Luna y Arellano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1528, and Hernando de Soto, who marched from Tampa Bay in 1539 as far as the Mississippi, found no riches, and attempts to settle, at San Miguel de Gualdape in 1526 and at Ochuse and Santa Elena under <b>Tristán de Luna y Arellano</b> in 1559, ended in failure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Detachments of <b>Tristán de Luna y Arellano</b>’s colony found Coosa much reduced in 1560, and Juan Pardo’s expeditions of 1566–1568 built small forts at Joara and other towns, which local people destroyed by 1568.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>When soldiers of the <b>Tristán de Luna</b> expedition returned in 1560, Coosa was already smaller and fighting rebellious tributaries.</blockquote><a href="/state/coosa-chiefdom">Coosa chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Borobia, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.67,-1.9&amp;year=1550" aria-label="Borobia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4504 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tristán de Luna y Arellano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4954 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4954 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.67,-1.9&amp;year=1550">What was here in 1550? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
